#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068433 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87636457-bdd5-53dd-9137-c070a4e12e84" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "360239d4-c271-52f3-8d25-ed47e7dd52f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10610" :question/problem "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $-2x^2+5x-1=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have $a=-2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(5)^{2}-4(-2)(-1) \\\\  & =25-8 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "f667bd43-407c-55a9-9821-67a0c1b11a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-10611" :question/problem "Consider the equation $-x^2-x+1=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-4(-1)(1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(\\mathcal{D}>0),$ the equation has two distinct solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "5d4530b7-c6df-583f-b98f-8a8f93203295" :question/math-academy-id "e-10612" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $x^2+2x+k=0$ has no real solutions." :question/worked-solution "The equation has no real solutions if its discriminant is negative. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(k) \\\\  & =4-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & <0 \\\\ 4-4k & <0 \\\\ 4 & <4k \\\\ 1 & <k \\\\ k & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have no real solutions if $k>1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adc75098-5640-5e81-a0bc-003fa20c090c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadratic Equations With Rational Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "b92b9a39-e29a-532f-ba2c-f935773d0bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10616" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $x^2+2x -2 = 0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-2.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(-2) \\\\  & =4+8 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $12$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. First, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cba47ec-7248-5b5f-b8bb-800bfc3a0781" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 3600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532061} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdf0be9-e0e1-56db-82b8-4ccd9f5bdbc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532214} {:answer/id #uuid "c25b4b4a-273b-533d-872e-af47c9ed53fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532348} {:answer/id #uuid "d22b8ac3-c049-5865-9013-008bea9a7215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532382} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dd366d-1802-52fa-b798-868adb32b32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dd366d-1802-52fa-b798-868adb32b32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\pm 60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532384} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f914387-42f8-52dc-abdc-1d71f70ce1d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24a9b789-abfb-5cbd-9e43-cbbe69e980a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-10318" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $-15{x}^{2}+kx-60=0$  has one distinct real solution." :question/worked-solution "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=-15,$ $b=k,$ and $c=-60,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(k)^{2}-4(-15)(-60) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-3\\,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3600 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =3600 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{3600} \\\\ k & =\\pm 60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\pm 60.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87636457-bdd5-53dd-9137-c070a4e12e84" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020f7ee6-efe4-5f13-82e0-365ef9f43144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531938} {:answer/id #uuid "42afc957-2dce-5d2c-b10d-dc81f6e20bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532071} {:answer/id #uuid "504b5177-c080-5fae-a299-aa4c42b860a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532106} {:answer/id #uuid "85925e09-d2b3-560f-8848-a483f325a3d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532234} {:answer/id #uuid "f67e2cda-3c4e-5f71-ace7-f25f9c242fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f67e2cda-3c4e-5f71-ace7-f25f9c242fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532468} :answer-field/id #uuid "33f8a14d-69ea-5b05-b75c-c3680e5e7284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b3abfcf-5108-5885-9c88-671c529d21e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112838" :question/problem "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $5{x}^{2}+6=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have $a=5,$ $b=0,$ and $c=6.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-4(5)(6) \\\\  & =0-120 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87636457-bdd5-53dd-9137-c070a4e12e84" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24384ea8-8c4f-51be-ad56-33bcc35835d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532009} {:answer/id #uuid "82ae67f2-ddd4-53c9-8d98-0466e6f78d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532228} {:answer/id #uuid "c4be73ba-9fef-5129-b52e-570a32eea822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-108" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532356} {:answer/id #uuid "d855cac1-b5cd-5f4b-9805-2d53a8c82519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532399} {:answer/id #uuid "ee74c235-9fa4-5f19-b15a-50d0c7905c4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82ae67f2-ddd4-53c9-8d98-0466e6f78d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532228} :answer-field/id #uuid "78b0844e-f95a-518b-84f1-546d37ff9d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "41b9a165-df20-54af-99cb-dea16d3c7585" :question/math-academy-id "q-112843" :question/problem "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $4(x-2)^{2}-8=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{2}-8 & =4({x}^{2}-4x+4)-8 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-16x+16-8 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-16x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $a=4,$ $b=-16,$ and $c=8.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-16)^{2}-4(4)(8) \\\\  & =256-128 \\\\  & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13108fc0-14f7-5be6-a079-66e6543471e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531969} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2943e9-482f-5384-a8a4-be57fc0e70b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532098} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a035c1-ca51-5895-887b-b811aaaf6e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532437} {:answer/id #uuid "f78e00a8-bc9a-5905-8191-42cd7f6c0168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532470} {:answer/id #uuid "fd76e629-d0fd-5091-9729-bc54997ceda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd76e629-d0fd-5091-9729-bc54997ceda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532490} :answer-field/id #uuid "14ed64de-3176-555a-be70-6011ce75968f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79550be8-05ba-52d9-a59a-1c06a72e87c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-112846" :question/problem "Consider the equation $-2{x}^{2}+6x-5=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-2,$ $b=6,$ and $c=-5,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={6}^{2}-4(-2)(-5) \\\\  & =36-40 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is negative $(D<0),$ the equation has no real solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dcb792e-2abd-5284-8521-8a30de4da730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532138} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccfd977-2d5c-5a24-a95d-40f927245322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532213} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9b6cbe-64a6-5894-afb0-5ac6cae08cb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532322} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d4e1e2-dde7-5878-b6ed-ace028ff302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532329} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5d739d-c5b9-59ad-949f-5db65c190d29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d4e1e2-dde7-5878-b6ed-ace028ff302a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532329} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d18037b-3674-5480-8ce7-b35031e52aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96eb4e16-d3f8-5bec-99cb-02da24948e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-112849" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-12x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=-12,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-12)^{2}-4(4)(9) \\\\  & =144-144 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adc75098-5640-5e81-a0bc-003fa20c090c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadratic Equations With Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9f5221-4063-5d13-89d0-9cbeae6dfa3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531959} {:answer/id #uuid "2f161efc-27e4-5e36-bbe3-20a7ed6e5cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532028} {:answer/id #uuid "38183de3-6469-5b98-995c-7988edd9d1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532047} {:answer/id #uuid "ac677453-135e-55a6-afd4-f9d0634419e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532319} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb0fa8a-aae6-5dd3-871e-f62b0a64d439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac677453-135e-55a6-afd4-f9d0634419e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532319} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2d3e179-11b4-52f5-bce3-6a401512952d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "389f1f33-a04d-5409-a2d5-8e337f759108" :question/math-academy-id "q-158003" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}+6x+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=4,$ $b=6,$ and $c=1.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(6)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =36-16 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $20$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adc75098-5640-5e81-a0bc-003fa20c090c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadratic Equations With Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e90531f-10e7-5a64-a37d-2ff16fbe4573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531960} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca03129-5630-59ae-856c-9aeac68e2746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531993} {:answer/id #uuid "870ca322-0deb-526f-8d2a-237cf9b7375c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532241} {:answer/id #uuid "8a985eb6-e820-5437-81f1-28b8193dc517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532248} {:answer/id #uuid "fabeb6ac-b3cb-5d6b-a30c-ac5b939b9949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "870ca322-0deb-526f-8d2a-237cf9b7375c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532241} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f49b58-68ca-5023-ae7f-9ab77db5a13c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9f98f1e-4801-5a10-97a0-297033319c24" :question/math-academy-id "q-158008" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation $2{x}^{2}-2x-2=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=-2.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2) \\\\  & =4+16 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $20$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc16e37-ecee-5679-a64a-218fee94f795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531955} {:answer/id #uuid "183b332e-d73d-5559-aad9-bd1b300be86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531982} {:answer/id #uuid "55f4fc92-aa88-5239-b750-208704cac86e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532121} {:answer/id #uuid "b51b432d-5224-5995-95d9-0472ce8e9b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532331} {:answer/id #uuid "b7dcd89c-6bd2-5e12-8812-c82738dd636d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k\\ge 15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b51b432d-5224-5995-95d9-0472ce8e9b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532331} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9816bbe-93b2-52b6-837d-9867d4d1aa47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10d98fee-4a02-5f93-813f-e8d53425cb38" :question/math-academy-id "q-44577" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $3{x}^{2}+12x+k=0$ has two distinct real solutions." :question/worked-solution "The equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is positive.\n\nWe have $a=3,$ $b=12,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={12}^{2}-4(3)(k) \\\\  & =144-12k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & >0 \\\\ 144-12k & >0 \\\\ 144 & >12k \\\\ k & <12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have two distinct real solutions if $k<12.$"}]}
